    "updated_at": "2023-01-26T01:50:05Z",
    "body": "I'd like to note that there are some edge cases that are being made weird when relying only on `requestAnimationFrame` (rAF) to commit to the placeholder canvas.  \r\n\r\n[Example 1](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/69137766/) and [related](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/73793109): That user wanted to record the stream from a placeholder canvas, on which they draw from a Worker. `captureStream()` can only be called from the main thread, but they didn't need to actually *present* the canvas in the page, nor did they really need their drawing frequency to be synced with the monitor's refresh rate, and thus bound to rAF. However in these circumstances, the new drawings were never committed to the placeholder canvas. `ctx.commit()` would have solved this use case (arguably a pulling MediaStream too).\r\n\r\n[Example 2](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/62546515): That user did create a Worker only to render one image and then killed the Worker as soon as it performed the drawing operations. This caused an issue where the drawings weren't committed to the placeholder canvas yet, because rAF didn't fire before the Worker got terminated. I know creating a Worker for just one drawing is a very bad design, but that same issue could have happened even in more usual circumstances, if the user doesn't wait for after the next painting frame before terminating the Worker. (Somehow Firefox's current implementation seems to be able to handle that one, I'm curious how it is done).\r\n\r\nI'm not saying we should absolutely keep `commit()` around just for these edge cases but it seems we should at least handle them in some specs words.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
