    "created_at": "2023-01-26T03:24:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-01-26T03:24:27Z",
    "body": "> There seems to be an assumption that if `commit` existed it would magically sync.\r\n\r\nYes that was the purpose of `commit()`, to synchronously grab the current image on the context so that it can be painted on the placeholder canvas. Which makes it indeed a bit of a shorthand for the long and tedious `postMessage(transferToImageBitmap());` in another thread `transferFromImageBitmap(evt.data.bitmap)`, with the added benefit that it shouldn't require the main thread to be free.\r\n\r\n> How does that compare to video?\r\n\r\nThis is mainly irrelevant. The point is not about having anything synced to some timer, the point is that the image never ever reaches the placeholder canvas nor the captured stream.\r\n\r\n> Example 2: sounds basically the same. If you want to synchronize, transfer an imagebitmap.\r\n\r\nThey don't want to synchronize, they want their drawings to be rendered on their placeholder canvas.\r\n\r\nUsing a bitmaprender might workaround these issues, just like waiting for `requestAnimationFrame`, but why should we go through such a complicated path? And anyway, the behavior using a placeholder canvas is not interoperable currently, it ought to at least make sense and be handled by the specs.\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
