    "created_at": "2023-01-26T09:51:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-01-26T09:52:43Z",
    "body": "> You exit the event and an implicit commit happens\r\n\r\nI don't think this is true. Currently you need to wait for a rendering opportunity for this commit to happen. In current specs texts for the Worker event loop it's if \"the user agent believes that it would benefit from having its rendering updated at this time\".  But terminating a worker also disregards all queued tasks*, so calling `drawOnCtx(); self.close()` would kill the Worker before it gets a chance to execute the implicit commit that's part of the \"update the rendering\" steps. *(Though to be fair it doesn't explicitly talk about animation frame callbacks, but I'm quite confident they are to be included).  \r\nThe point is that, in this PR, we need to handle these edge cases that `commit` could handle but that the current model doesn't. This might be by forcing the UA to enter the \"update the rendering\" steps of the Worker's event loop when it's being terminated, this might be another solution, but it's needed. ",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
